M="biochem-metab-2"
SUB="Metabolism"
T={
"aa":[("Which two amino acids are purely ketogenic?",["Leucine and lysine","Valine and isoleucine","Alanine and glycine","Methionine and threonine"],0,"Both begin with L. Every other amino acid can contribute carbon to glucose."),
 ("Which amino acid is considered essential in growing children but not strictly in healthy adults?",["Arginine","Alanine","Glutamate","Serine"],0,"Histidine and arginine are conditionally essential during growth."),
 ("During prolonged starvation, glucogenic amino acids are important because they supply:",["Carbon skeletons for gluconeogenesis","Nitrogen for disposal by the urea cycle","Acetyl-CoA for making ketone bodies","NADPH for reductive biosynthesis"],0,"Their carbon enters as pyruvate or TCA intermediates. Ketogenic amino acids cannot be converted to glucose.",2)],
"nh3":[("Which reaction actually releases free ammonia from amino acids?",["Oxidative deamination by glutamate dehydrogenase","Transamination by alanine aminotransferase","Transamination by aspartate aminotransferase","Amide formation by glutamine synthetase"],0,"Transamination only funnels nitrogen into glutamate; deamination releases it."),
 ("Ammonia is carried safely in the blood mainly as:",["Glutamine","Urea","Aspartate","Creatinine"],0,"Glutamine synthetase traps ammonia; it is the most abundant free amino acid in plasma."),
 ("Why does hyperammonaemia affect the brain so severely?",["The brain has no urea cycle and must trap ammonia as glutamine","Ammonia directly dissolves myelin","The blood-brain barrier concentrates ammonia actively","Ammonia inhibits the sodium-potassium pump"],0,"Brain detoxification depends on glutamine synthetase, and the process drains alpha-ketoglutarate from the TCA cycle.",3)],
"urea":[("The rate-determining enzyme of the urea cycle is:",["Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I","Ornithine transcarbamylase","Argininosuccinate lyase","Arginase"],0,"It requires N-acetylglutamate as an obligatory allosteric activator."),
 ("Where do the two nitrogen atoms of urea come from?",["Free ammonia and aspartate","Glutamine and glutamate","Aspartate and alanine","Free ammonia and glutamine"],0,"The first enters via CPS I, the second is donated by aspartate in the argininosuccinate synthetase step."),
 ("A patient eats a very high-protein meal. Which change allows the urea cycle to accelerate appropriately?",["More N-acetylglutamate activates CPS I","Arginase is induced by dietary arginine","Ornithine leaves the mitochondrion faster","Fumarate directly activates the cycle"],0,"N-acetylglutamate is made from glutamate and acetyl-CoA, so it rises with protein intake and matches urea output to the nitrogen load.",3)],
"hyperam":[("Lactulose lowers blood ammonia by:",["Acidifying the gut so ammonia is trapped","Binding ammonia directly in the bloodstream","Inducing the hepatic urea cycle enzymes","Increasing renal excretion of ammonia"],0,"The charged ammonium ion cannot be reabsorbed and is lost in stool."),
 ("Which drug lowers ammonia by reducing gut bacteria that generate it?",["Rifaximin","Lactulose","Sodium benzoate","Phenylbutyrate"],0,"It is a poorly absorbed antibiotic used alongside lactulose."),
 ("A neonate is lethargic and tachypnoeic on day 3. Ammonia is 450 micromol/L, and arterial pH and anion gap are both normal. Which group of disorders is most likely?",["A urea cycle defect","An organic acidaemia","A fatty acid oxidation defect","A mitochondrial myopathy"],0,"High ammonia with a normal pH points to the urea cycle. Organic acidaemias raise ammonia too, but with a wide anion gap metabolic acidosis.",3)],
"otc":[("OTC deficiency is inherited as:",["X-linked recessive","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant","Mitochondrial"],0,"It is the only X-linked urea cycle disorder and the most common of the group."),
 ("Orotic acid appears in the urine in OTC deficiency because:",["Carbamoyl phosphate is diverted into pyrimidine synthesis","Orotate is a normal end product of the urea cycle","Arginine is degraded to orotate in the liver","Ammonia is converted to orotate by the kidney"],0,"Carbamoyl phosphate spills from mitochondria into the cytosol and feeds the pyrimidine pathway."),
 ("A boy has orotic aciduria. Which finding distinguishes OTC deficiency from hereditary orotic aciduria?",["A raised blood ammonia level","The presence of orotate crystals","An elevated serum arginine","A low urinary urea"],0,"Lippincott makes this the key discriminator: ammonia is high in OTC deficiency and normal in hereditary orotic aciduria, which instead has a megaloblastic anaemia unresponsive to B12 and folate.",3)],
"aader":[("Tetrahydrobiopterin is required for the synthesis of:",["Catecholamines and serotonin","Heme and the porphyrins","Glutathione and GABA","Urea, creatine and NO"],0,"It is the cofactor for phenylalanine, tyrosine and tryptophan hydroxylases."),
 ("Which amino acid is the precursor of both serotonin and niacin?",["Tryptophan","Tyrosine","Histidine","Glycine"],0,"Diverting it toward serotonin can leave too little for niacin, producing pellagra-like features."),
 ("A patient with carcinoid syndrome develops dermatitis, diarrhoea and confusion. The mechanism is:",["Tryptophan is diverted away from niacin synthesis","Histidine is excessively decarboxylated to histamine","Tyrosine is shunted into melanin synthesis instead","Glycine is consumed by porphyrin synthesis"],0,"The tumour consumes tryptophan, so NAD+ synthesis falls and a pellagra-like picture appears.",3)],
"pku":[("The enzyme deficient in classic phenylketonuria is:",["Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Tyrosinase","Homogentisate oxidase","Cystathionine beta-synthase"],0,"Phenylalanine can no longer be converted to tyrosine, so tyrosine becomes essential."),
 ("Why must tyrosine be supplemented in PKU?",["It can no longer be made from phenylalanine","It is lost excessively in the urine","It is destroyed by the phenylketones","It is not absorbed from the gut"],0,"Tyrosine becomes a conditionally essential amino acid in these patients."),
 ("An infant with high phenylalanine does not improve despite strict dietary restriction, and develops a movement disorder. Which explanation fits best?",["A defect in tetrahydrobiopterin recycling","Poor dietary adherence by the family","A coexisting deficiency of tyrosinase","Undiagnosed maternal phenylketonuria"],0,"BH4 is also needed for tyrosine and tryptophan hydroxylases, so dopamine and serotonin synthesis fail. Diet alone cannot fix this; BH4 and neurotransmitter precursors are required.",3)],
"tyr":[("The urine of a patient with alkaptonuria darkens on standing because of:",["Oxidised homogentisic acid","Excess melanin pigment","Breakdown of myoglobin","Porphobilinogen build-up"],0,"Homogentisate oxidase is deficient, so homogentisic acid accumulates and oxidises on exposure to air."),
 ("Ochronosis in alkaptonuria refers to pigment deposition in:",["Cartilage and connective tissue","Liver and splenic sinusoids","Renal tubular epithelium","Peripheral nerve sheaths"],0,"It produces dark ears and sclerae and early degenerative arthritis."),
 ("Albinism increases skin cancer risk mainly because:",["Melanin that absorbs ultraviolet light is missing","DNA repair enzymes are also defective","Tyrosine accumulates and is carcinogenic","Immune surveillance of skin is impaired"],0,"The defect is in tyrosinase or tyrosine transport, so melanin cannot be made. DNA repair is normal, unlike in xeroderma pigmentosum.",2)],
"msud":[("Maple syrup urine disease is caused by deficiency of:",["Branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase and its cofactor","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase in mitochondria","Cystathionine beta-synthase in the liver"],0,"Isoleucine, leucine and valine and their ketoacids accumulate."),
 ("Which vitamin is the cofactor of the enzyme deficient in MSUD?",["Thiamine","Biotin","Cobalamin","Pyridoxine"],0,"The complex uses thiamine pyrophosphate, as do pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase."),
 ("Which branched-chain amino acid is most responsible for the neurological damage in MSUD?",["Leucine","Valine","Isoleucine","All three equally"],0,"Leucine and its ketoacid are the most neurotoxic, so dietary management restricts leucine most tightly.",2)],
"hcy":[("The commonest cause of homocystinuria is deficiency of:",["Cystathionine beta-synthase","Methionine synthase","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","Homogentisate oxidase"],0,"Homocysteine and methionine rise while cysteine falls."),
 ("Which amino acid becomes essential in cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency?",["Cysteine","Methionine","Glycine","Serine"],0,"Cysteine is normally made from methionine via homocysteine, and that route is blocked."),
 ("A tall thin 14-year-old has downward lens dislocation, a thrombotic stroke and intellectual disability. Which feature best separates this from Marfan syndrome?",["The lens dislocates downward, with thrombosis","The tall stature with long thin limbs","The presence of a marked scoliosis","The generalised joint hypermobility"],0,"Homocystinuria dislocates the lens downward and inward and causes thrombosis; Marfan dislocates upward and outward with no thrombotic tendency.",3)],
"cystin":[("The defect in cystinuria is in:",["A transporter in kidney and intestine","A lysosomal degradative enzyme","A mitochondrial membrane carrier","An enzyme of the hepatic urea cycle"],0,"It affects cystine, ornithine, arginine and lysine, which share one transporter."),
 ("Which crystals are characteristic in the urine?",["Hexagonal","Envelope-shaped","Coffin-lid","Needle-shaped"],0,"Hexagonal cystine crystals are nearly diagnostic."),
 ("Despite losing four amino acids in the urine, patients with cystinuria have symptoms only from stones. Why?",["Peptide absorption still supplies those amino acids","The urinary losses are far too small to matter","The liver can synthesise all four of them","Only cystine is actually lost in the urine"],0,"Di- and tripeptide transporters are intact, so nutritional deficiency does not develop; only the poorly soluble cystine precipitates.",3)],
"organic":[("Propionyl-CoA is produced from all of the following EXCEPT:",["Leucine","Valine","Odd-chain fatty acids","Methionine"],0,"The sources are valine, odd-chain fatty acids, methionine, isoleucine and threonine. Leucine is purely ketogenic."),
 ("Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase requires which cofactor?",["Vitamin B12","Biotin","Thiamine","Pyridoxine"],0,"Propionyl-CoA carboxylase, the step before it, requires biotin instead."),
 ("A neonate has vomiting, lethargy, a wide anion gap metabolic acidosis and a mildly raised ammonia. Which feature points to an organic acidaemia rather than a urea cycle defect?",["The metabolic acidosis","The raised ammonia","The lethargy","The age at presentation"],0,"Urea cycle disorders raise ammonia with a normal pH, whereas organic acidaemias produce an acidosis with a wide anion gap.",3)],
}
